.Dd July 10, 2026
.Dt XTC_STREAM 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_stream_create ,
.Nm xtc_stream_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_stream_next ,
.Nm xtc_stream_map ,
.Nm xtc_stream_filter ,
.Nm xtc_stream_for_each ,
.Nm xtc_stream_from_demand
.Nd lazy pull-based async streams with combinators
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libxtc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_stream.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stream_create "xtc_stream_next_fn next" "void *ctx" "xtc_stream_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_stream_destroy "xtc_stream_t *s"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stream_next "xtc_stream_t *s" "void **out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stream_map "xtc_stream_t *s" "void *(*fn)(void *, void *)" "void *user" "xtc_stream_t **out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stream_filter "xtc_stream_t *s" "int (*pred)(void *, void *)" "void *user" "xtc_stream_t **out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stream_for_each "xtc_stream_t *s" "int (*fn)(void *, void *)" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_stream_from_demand "xtc_chan_demand_t *ch" "xtc_stream_t **out"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An async stream is a lazy, pull-based sequence of values -- the Elixir
.Ql Stream
and Rust
.Ql Iterator
shape.  A stream is just a
.Fn next
function that yields the next value or signals end-of-stream, wrapped
so combinators can layer transformations without materializing the
whole sequence.  Pull-based composition means a slow downstream stage
exerts backpressure simply by pulling more slowly, which pairs directly
with the demand channel
.Pq Xr xtc_chan 3 .
.Pp
Values are
.Vt void * ;
the stream never copies or frees them.  A combinator's transform decides
ownership.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_stream_create
builds a base stream from a
.Fn next
function and its context.
.Fn xtc_stream_next
pulls one value: it writes
.Fa *out
and returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
at end-of-stream,
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
if no value is ready yet but the stream is not finished (retry), or
another
.Dv XTC_E_*
on error.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_stream_map
returns a stream in which each pulled value
.Fa v
becomes
.Fn fn v user ;
.Fn xtc_stream_filter
returns a stream that skips values for which
.Fn pred v user
is 0.  Both are lazy and both OWN the source stream, so destroying the
combinator destroys the source; destroy only the outermost stream.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_stream_for_each
drains a stream, calling
.Fn fn v user
for each value until end-of-stream.  A nonzero
.Fn fn
return stops early and is returned; an
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
from the source is retried, so use
.Fn xtc_stream_for_each
only with a source that will eventually produce or finish.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_stream_from_demand
adapts a demand channel into a stream: each pull grants one unit of
demand and returns the next buffered item.  It does not own the channel.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
All functions except
.Fn xtc_stream_destroy
return
.Dv XTC_OK
or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code (see
.Fn xtc_stream_next
for its stream-specific codes).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_stream_destroy
returns no value; it recursively destroys any wrapped source stream but
never touches the demand channel of a
.Fn xtc_stream_from_demand
stream.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* double the even numbers coming off a demand channel */
static int is_even(void *v, void *u) { (void)u; return (*(int*)v & 1)==0; }
static void *dbl(void *v, void *u) { (void)u; *(int*)v *= 2; return v; }
static int print_it(void *v, void *u) { (void)u; printf("%d\\n",*(int*)v); return 0; }

xtc_stream_t *s, *f, *m;
xtc_stream_from_demand(ch, &s);
xtc_stream_filter(s, is_even, NULL, &f);
xtc_stream_map(f, dbl, NULL, &m);
xtc_stream_for_each(m, print_it, NULL);
xtc_stream_destroy(m);   /* frees m -> f -> s */
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_chan 3 ,
.Xr xtc_launch 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
